WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.597
Please welcome the one and only, Joseph Gordon-Levitt.

2
00:00:03.597 --> 00:00:05.840
(upbeat lively music)

3
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.021
Oh my God! I'm really excited.

4
00:00:09.021 --> 00:00:10.523
Hi, everybody!

5
00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:14.307
(audience cheering)

6
00:00:14.307 --> 00:00:15.579
Thank you for having me.

7
00:00:15.579 --> 00:00:18.829
Oh my gosh! I'm so excited.
Me too.

8
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.160
I've loved you my whole life.

9
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.300
That's how I feel about you, Drew.

10
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.330
Honestly, it's really true.

11
00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.850
Thank you.
Yeah.

12
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.590
It's not easy.

13
00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.020
I remember saying to my daughters,

14
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.970
they were in the back of the car and we were talking

15
00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.827
about someone, and I said,

16
00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:38.467
"You know, it's hard to grow up, at all,"

17
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.850
"let alone in front of everybody."

18
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.610
I had done TV movies, feature films,

19
00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.310
commercials, before ET.

20
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:47.815
I'd already been at it for years.

21
00:00:47.815 --> 00:00:48.648
Yeah.

22
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:49.800
And you had too before "Third Rock".

23
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:50.633
That's true, yeah.

24
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:51.980
'Cause I started working when I was six,

25
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.910
and I started Third Rock from the summer when I was 13.

26
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.700
So how did you learn boundaries?

27
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.110
'Cause even as a parent now,

28
00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:00.567
when my kids were young and people will be like,

29
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:01.750
"Well, you know, boundaries."

30
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.350
And I was like, "No".

31
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.840
I didn't have any as a kid, I wasn't in a job that did,

32
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.250
my parents didn't.

33
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.970 line:15%
My wife and I talk about boundaries a lot,

34
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.970 line:15%
that it's important to give lots of playroom,

35
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.520
but it's also important that the boundaries don't move.

36
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:20.540
We read this study.

37
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.390
It was a really interesting thing

38
00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.510
where they conducted this study with rats, right?

39
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:29.160
And they put a wall around them,

40
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.520
and then they had another group of rats

41
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.080
that didn't have a wall.

42
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.420
And the rats that didn't have a wall bunched closer together

43
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:38.970
and were kind of much more nervous,

44
00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.620
and didn't play as adventurously,

45
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.130
as the rats who had a wall.

46
00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.930
Because if you know where the wall is,

47
00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.580
if you know where the boundary is,

48
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.740
then you're free to play within those boundaries,

49
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.430
if you don't know where the boundary is,

50
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:54.878
then you're kind of uncertain the whole time,

51
00:01:54.878 --> 00:01:57.220
you don't know where the boundary is.

52
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.720 line:15%
The walls do make you feel safer.

53
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.210 line:15%
And I love that analogy, it will forever live with me.

54
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.390 line:15%
Thank you.
Oh, good.

55
00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:04.557 line:15%
That's so perfect.
I'm so happy.

56
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:05.518 line:15%
(audience applauding)

57
00:02:05.518 --> 00:02:06.745 line:15%
I am happy.

58
00:02:06.745 --> 00:02:10.400 line:15%
Well, there is a story about a casting director.

59
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.233
Oh, yeah.

60
00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:12.980
And I was wondering if you'd tell it

61
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.100
because it seems like it was a moment

62
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.670
that may have changed your life.

63
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.670
Yeah, it really did.

64
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.030 line:15%
This is a story about the very first audition

65
00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.360 line:15%
I ever went on.

66
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.058 line:15%
It was for a Red Lobster commercial.

67
00:02:26.058 --> 00:02:27.870 line:15%
(Joseph laughing)

68
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.320
And I had to go in and sing and I got in there and froze,

69
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.130
couldn't do it.

70
00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.630
And once I had kind of finished tumbling my way

71
00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.040
through the song, the casting director looked me

72
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.080
right in the eye and said, "That was great, thank you."

73
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.430
And what I love about this is

74
00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:46.980
that he didn't have to do that.

75
00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.790
And it would've destroyed me at six years old,

76
00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:50.960
and I probably never would've gone

77
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.340
on another audition again 'cause I would've been traumatized

78
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.950
and I wouldn't have become an actor.

79
00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.350
But because this guy took an extra couple of minutes

80
00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:02.040
in his day to be kind, now I get to be here

81
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.615
and doing this and talking to you.

82
00:03:03.615 --> 00:03:05.581
(audience cheering)

83
00:03:05.581 --> 00:03:06.414
I don't know him.

84
00:03:06.414 --> 00:03:10.263
I don't know who it was, but I'm so grateful to that guy.

85
00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.210
Where is he?
I don't know.

86
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.045
Help me find him.

87
00:03:14.045 --> 00:03:15.109
I would love that.
I know, right.

88
00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:17.059
If you're that man, if you're the man

89
00:03:18.957 --> 00:03:22.040
who set the example of how we should all treat each other

90
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.760
and changed the trajectory of Joseph Gordon-Levitt's life,

91
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.264
making all of our lives better, please find us.

92
00:03:29.264 --> 00:03:31.733
Yeah.
The drewbarrymoreshow.com.

93
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.680
Joseph Gordon-Levitt, Red Lobster commercial.

94
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:35.889
He was the guy.

95
00:03:35.889 --> 00:03:38.560
You changed his life and you changed all of our life for it.

96
00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.070
Thank you for sharing that story.

97
00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.930
Yeah.
May we all remember it

98
00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.710
because how we treat each other

99
00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:47.690
has the most tremendous effects on the rest of our lives.

100
00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.450
Which leads me directly to asking you about Steven Spielberg

101
00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.900
And working with him.
Yeah.

102
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.370
May I ask what your experience working with him was like?

103
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.700
And you were about to go into directing yourself?

104
00:03:58.700 --> 00:03:59.610
That was the thing.

105
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.030
Yeah, I was about to direct my first feature film, Don John,

106
00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.080
when we shot "Lincoln", when I got to work with him.

107
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.270
And so, obviously, I have the opportunity

108
00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.000
to observe one of the greats.

109
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:10.833
Yeah.

110
00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:12.360
I was just watching him like a Hawk the whole time.

111
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.540
And one of the things that I noticed was,

112
00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.820
he didn't have a shot lists.

113
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.010
And at one point, I actually worked up the courage

114
00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:25.010
to say like, "Hey, Steven, do you have a shot list?"

115
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.897
And he was like, "Well, no. Here, lemme show you."

116
00:04:27.897 --> 00:04:30.167
"I like to just watch what you guys do first."

117
00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:31.150
"And then I figure it out."

118
00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.467
I'm like, "You just figure it out,"

119
00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:36.748
"these Spielbergian cinematic shots that you make."

120
00:04:36.748 --> 00:04:38.770
He hasn't listed them, he hasn't story boarded them.

121
00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.000
He can just kind of whip them up.

122
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.637
He watches what the actors do and he's like,

123
00:04:42.637 --> 00:04:44.680
"The cameras should go here."

124
00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.170
He can just see it.

125
00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.077
Now, switching gears a little bit.

126
00:04:49.077 --> 00:04:52.570
"Super Pumped: The Battle for Uber",

127
00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.017
I am five episodes in.

128
00:04:55.017 --> 00:04:57.107
Oh, nice.
I'm so into it.

129
00:04:57.107 --> 00:04:58.673
Oh good.
Oh my God!

130
00:04:58.673 --> 00:05:00.050
How do you like it?

131
00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:05.050
Tell me how you play someone so insatiable?

132
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.470
It's so good.

133
00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.445
Travis Kalanick, who co-founded Uber,

134
00:05:10.445 --> 00:05:14.100
he made the fastest growing start-up in history.

135
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.080
There's something really admirable about that.

136
00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.380
He changed the world.

137
00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.770
Yeah, the question is, what was the cost,

138
00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.160
and who had to pay that costs?

139
00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.140
Because a lot of the folks, especially, the drivers

140
00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:30.130
who are working with Uber or for Uber,

141
00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.490
the employees of Uber, they really kind of got a bad deal.

142
00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:38.450
And why can't we reward companies

143
00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:42.510
who do great things but don't have to step on people,

144
00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.326
even if they're a little bit less successful?

145
00:05:44.326 --> 00:05:45.159 line:15%
(audience applauding)

146
00:05:45.159 --> 00:05:48.326 line:15%
(upbeat lively music)

